1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,131
May eighth, nineteen forty-five.

2
00:00:02,580 --> 00:00:04,780
The war in Europe is
over. You survived it.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,040
So you pack your bag and
wait for the ride home.

4
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:11,720
But nobody lets you leave. Because
to go home, you need a number.

5
00:00:11,812 --> 00:00:14,840
Eighty-five. And you are about
to find out if you have it.

6
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:20,440
Here is the Army's problem. On victory
day, about three million Americans were in

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:24,500
Europe. Across the world, more
than seven million served overseas.

8
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:27,140
Everyone wanted the first ship home.

9
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:29,560
But there were not nearly enough ships.

10
00:00:30,070 --> 00:00:32,160
And the war against
Japan still needed men.

11
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:35,260
Nobody wanted it more
than the men themselves.

12
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:40,749
General Maxwell Taylor asked the hundred
and first Airborne where they wanted to go

13
00:00:40,769 --> 00:00:43,180
next. The men shouted one word. Home.

14
00:00:44,180 --> 00:00:48,780
So who goes first? Back in nineteen
forty-three, General George Marshall had

15
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,840
put planners on exactly this question.

16
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:55,700
Their answer was announced on
May tenth, nineteen forty-five.

17
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:57,000
Just two days after victory.

18
00:00:57,439 --> 00:00:58,917
It had an official name.

19
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:00,880
The Adjusted Service Rating Score.

20
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,098
Soldiers just called it the point system.

21
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:05,560
The idea sounded fair.

22
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:08,540
Those who had fought longest and
hardest should go home first.

23
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:14,302
But there was a catch. Your points were
counted only up to May twelfth, nineteen

24
00:01:14,322 --> 00:01:19,240
forty-five. Whatever happened to you after
that day did not count. At least, not yet.

25
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,820
So let's do what every soldier
in Europe did that week.

26
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:25,420
Grab a pencil. Let's count your points.

27
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,120
Rule one. Every month in
uniform is worth one point.

28
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:34,420
The count starts in September nineteen
forty, when the peacetime draft became

29
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:39,800
law. Rule two. Every month
overseas earns one more point.

30
00:01:39,820 --> 00:01:42,920
So a month in England or
France is worth double.

31
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:48,220
Rule three. Every campaign star
or combat medal adds five points.

32
00:01:49,220 --> 00:01:52,420
That includes the Bronze Star, the
Silver Star and the Purple Heart.

33
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:56,244
Rule four is the one
that surprised everyone.

34
00:01:56,540 --> 00:01:58,740
Every child under eighteen
adds twelve points.

35
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,000
Up to three children.

36
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:04,220
Being married gave you nothing.
Your age gave you nothing.

37
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:07,260
But a child back home was
worth a full year in uniform.

38
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:10,300
The bar was eighty-five for enlisted men.

39
00:02:10,428 --> 00:02:11,700
Eighty-four meant you stayed.

40
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:15,280
Women in the Women's Army
Corps needed forty-four.

41
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:20,160
Cartoonist Bill Mauldin drew a soldier
greeting a child as a wonderful little

42
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:21,340
twelve-point rascal.

43
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:24,582
Now let's play. Meet three soldiers.

44
00:02:24,602 --> 00:02:26,120
They are not real people.

45
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:28,760
They are typical cases,
built from the real rules.

46
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:31,640
Card one. The father.

47
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:33,320
A sergeant, thirty years old.

48
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,720
Three years in uniform.
Two years overseas.

49
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:38,960
Three campaign stars. Two kids at home.

50
00:02:39,750 --> 00:02:43,453
Thirty-six, plus twenty-four,
plus fifteen, plus twenty-four.

51
00:02:43,473 --> 00:02:45,500
Ninety-nine points. He is going home.

52
00:02:46,580 --> 00:02:49,891
Card two. You. Drafted in
January nineteen forty-three.

53
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:52,840
That's twenty-eight months,
so twenty-eight points.

54
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:56,683
You shipped overseas in
May nineteen forty-four.

55
00:02:56,703 --> 00:02:57,760
Twelve more points.

56
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:00,520
Then you fought from Normandy
all the way into Germany.

57
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,640
That's five campaign
stars. Twenty-five points.

58
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,410
No medals, no kids. Your
total is sixty-five.

59
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:10,625
Card three. The kid. Nineteen years old.

60
00:03:10,645 --> 00:03:15,780
Drafted in the summer of nineteen
forty-four, he arrived in December, just

61
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,620
in time for the Battle of the Bulge.

62
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:21,933
Ten points for service.
Five for months overseas.

63
00:03:21,953 --> 00:03:25,140
Fifteen for three campaign
stars. Thirty points total.

64
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:29,800
He was not alone. Private
Walter Anderson wrote home.

65
00:03:30,100 --> 00:03:33,780
I only have twenty-eight points, so there
isn't much chance of me getting home.

66
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:38,840
So the line was clear. Fathers
and long-serving veterans first.

67
00:03:39,200 --> 00:03:41,220
Everyone else would
wait. But wait for what?

68
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,131
Because the war was not over.

69
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:48,460
Japan was still fighting. And the Army
was planning to invade Japan itself.

70
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:54,318
The first landings were scheduled for
November first, nineteen forty-five.

71
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,840
The Army needed men for
it. Experienced men.

72
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:03,240
And planners knew the price. A January
nineteen forty-five Army study expected

73
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:07,080
about forty-three thousand replacements
a month for the dead and wounded.

74
00:04:08,260 --> 00:04:11,060
So every unit in Europe got
sorted into four groups.

75
00:04:11,980 --> 00:04:13,700
Some would stay as occupation troops.

76
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,380
Some would go home and disband.

77
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:21,200
And some would go to the Pacific. About
one million soldiers in Europe were slated

78
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:22,660
for the war against Japan.

79
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:26,424
Look at your card again.
Sixty-five points.

80
00:04:26,444 --> 00:04:28,360
Battle-tested, young, no kids.

81
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:30,400
You are exactly the soldier they want.

82
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:35,580
Then the shuffle began. High-point
men were moved into units going home.

83
00:04:35,757 --> 00:04:37,220
Low-point men were moved out.

84
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:42,890
In the Twenty-Eighth Infantry Division,
one in five enlisted men changed in a

85
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:45,780
single week. Your squad
dissolves around you.

86
00:04:46,420 --> 00:04:50,480
Even the father was not safe. If his
skills were needed, the Army could hold

87
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:51,900
him up to six more months.

88
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:58,200
Boredom set in. Sergeant Gerald Raftery
wrote about how monotonous and pointless

89
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:00,540
this whole existence over here has become.

90
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:04,260
And for one division, the
Pacific stopped being a rumor.

91
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:09,160
The Eighty-Sixth Infantry Division
sailed home and reached New York on June

92
00:05:09,180 --> 00:05:10,270
seventeenth.

93
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,399
They were finally home.

94
00:05:12,620 --> 00:05:14,100
But it was only a stopover.

95
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:17,300
The division was being retrained
for the war against Japan.

96
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:21,040
Meanwhile, the high-point men
headed for the coast of France.

97
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:23,880
But not straight to a
port. To a tent city.

98
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:29,240
In northern France, the Army ran a string
of camps named after cigarette brands.

99
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:32,880
Lucky Strike. Old Gold.
Philip Morris. Chesterfield.

100
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:35,989
They were called the cigarette camps.

101
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:39,000
The brand names were code names,
chosen mainly for security.

102
00:05:40,040 --> 00:05:43,340
The biggest, Camp Lucky Strike, could
hold fifty-eight thousand soldiers.

103
00:05:44,440 --> 00:05:46,740
A city of canvas, waiting
for one thing. A ship.

104
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:50,860
It was also the main stop for
freed American prisoners of war.

105
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:55,620
More than seventy-three thousand recovered
prisoners passed through Lucky Strike.

106
00:05:56,700 --> 00:05:59,080
Because ships were the real bottleneck.

107
00:05:59,100 --> 00:06:01,520
The war against Japan
had first claim on them.

108
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:04,520
In May, only ninety
thousand soldiers got home.

109
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:07,440
Everyone else waited for weeks, or months.

110
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:09,800
So the government got creative.

111
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:12,820
It turned three hundred cargo
ships into troop transports.

112
00:06:14,040 --> 00:06:16,668
It even got the British liner Queen Mary.

113
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:19,080
The price was ten cargo ships in exchange.

114
00:06:19,620 --> 00:06:22,540
The biggest liners squeezed
in up to fifteen thousand men.

115
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:28,266
At Lucky Strike, men got seventy-two
hours of warning before departure.

116
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:30,400
After that, nobody could leave the area.

117
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:35,160
But for you, with sixty-five points,
there was no warning. And no ship.

118
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,270
Then, in August, everything changed.

119
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:42,180
Two atomic bombs. And on August
fifteenth, Japan announced its surrender.

120
00:06:43,320 --> 00:06:45,018
The invasion was cancelled.

121
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:49,400
Overnight, the plan to send a million
men from Europe to the Pacific was dead.

122
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:52,360
But not for the Eighty-Sixth.

123
00:06:52,380 --> 00:06:56,980
Nine days after Japan's surrender, the
division sailed from San Francisco anyway.

124
00:06:57,650 --> 00:06:59,260
Destination, the Philippines.

125
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:01,540
At home, the pressure exploded.

126
00:07:02,148 --> 00:07:04,140
Families wanted their men back now.

127
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:06,840
Wives formed groups called
Bring Back Daddy clubs.

128
00:07:08,260 --> 00:07:11,460
Congress was buried in
letters. So the Army gave in.

129
00:07:11,740 --> 00:07:15,770
Right after victory over Japan, the
bar dropped from eighty-five to eighty.

130
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:21,680
Eisenhower was ordered to ship one million
seven hundred sixteen thousand men home by

131
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:27,220
the end of January. And the Navy
joined in. Warships became ferries.

132
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:31,540
Aircraft carriers filled their hangar
decks with bunks, three to five beds high.

133
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:37,200
On September sixth, Admiral Ernest King
cleared combat ships to carry troops home.

134
00:07:38,240 --> 00:07:42,640
By the autumn, the Atlantic sealift peaked
at four hundred thirty thousand soldiers a

135
00:07:42,660 --> 00:07:48,440
month. The new carrier Lake Champlain was
fitted with bunks for thirty-three hundred

136
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:52,660
troops. In December, nearly seven hundred
thousand came home from the Pacific.

137
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:56,820
So now the father was
gone. The bar was falling.

138
00:07:57,080 --> 00:07:59,650
But eighty was still out of
reach for your sixty-five.

139
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:03,140
There was another problem.
Somebody had to stay.

140
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:06,300
Germany, Austria and Japan
needed occupation troops.

141
00:08:07,220 --> 00:08:09,920
But the draft had been cut to
fifty thousand men a month.

142
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,720
That was not enough to
replace the men leaving.

143
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:17,660
So on January fourth, nineteen forty-six,
the War Department hit the brakes.

144
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:20,860
Discharges would now take
six months, not three.

145
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:24,740
For soldiers already promised a
ship, this felt like betrayal.

146
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:28,440
So they did something almost
unthinkable. They protested.

147
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:33,640
On Christmas Day, about four thousand
soldiers in Manila protested a cancelled

148
00:08:33,660 --> 00:08:39,120
ship. On January sixth, as many as twenty
thousand marched on Army headquarters.

149
00:08:40,340 --> 00:08:45,080
The protests spread to Guam, Japan,
France, Germany, Austria, India, Korea and

150
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:50,180
England. In London, about five hundred
soldiers confronted Eleanor Roosevelt.

151
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:55,500
In Army mailrooms in Manila and Tokyo,
soldiers stamped letters home with a

152
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:57,960
slogan. No boats, no votes.

153
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,768
Protesting in uniform
could mean a court-martial.

154
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:06,060
But Eisenhower, now the Army's Chief of
Staff, recommended no mass punishment.

155
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:08,660
His diagnosis was two words.

156
00:09:09,560 --> 00:09:12,020
Acute homesickness. He
banned further protests.

157
00:09:12,400 --> 00:09:14,520
But the Army also sped
up the system again.

158
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,620
It also tried to make
staying more attractive.

159
00:09:18,640 --> 00:09:21,720
Occupation troops could buy a
seventeen-day tour of Europe for

160
00:09:21,740 --> 00:09:24,010
twenty-five to thirty-five dollars.

161
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:27,600
So, would you have made it
home? Let's run the final count.

162
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:31,325
The bar kept falling. Seventy by October.

163
00:09:31,345 --> 00:09:32,573
Sixty by November.

164
00:09:32,593 --> 00:09:35,160
By December nineteenth, it was fifty.

165
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:37,760
Your sixty-five clears
the bar in November.

166
00:09:38,760 --> 00:09:41,260
You ride a truck to a cigarette
camp. You wait for a ship.

167
00:09:42,340 --> 00:09:45,080
Depending on your ship,
you are home that winter.

168
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:49,252
The European part of the sealift was
essentially finished by February nineteen

169
00:09:49,272 --> 00:09:53,180
forty-six. The father?
He was among the first.

170
00:09:53,420 --> 00:09:55,620
Home in the summer, holding
his twelve-point kids.

171
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:59,820
And the kid with thirty points?
He never reached the bar.

172
00:10:00,100 --> 00:10:03,380
In June nineteen forty-six, the
Army scrapped the points entirely.

173
00:10:04,620 --> 00:10:06,540
From then on, two years
of service was enough.

174
00:10:07,460 --> 00:10:11,400
For the kid, drafted in the summer of
nineteen forty-four, that meant the summer

175
00:10:11,420 --> 00:10:13,280
of nineteen forty-six.

176
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:17,700
That's the real answer. Eighty-five
was never the finish line.

177
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:19,980
It was a place in a line that kept moving.

178
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:25,960
The points decided the order. Ships, Japan
and the need for occupation troops decided

179
00:10:25,980 --> 00:10:26,980
the speed.

180
00:10:27,660 --> 00:10:32,146
The last troops from the Pacific arrived
home in September nineteen forty-six.

181
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:34,120
More than a year after the war ended.

182
00:10:35,120 --> 00:10:39,160
In two years, the military shrank from
more than twelve million people to about

183
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:40,180
one and a half million.

184
00:10:41,420 --> 00:10:45,360
And every veteran who came home got a
small gold-colored pin for the lapel.

185
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:48,780
It showed an eagle. The men
thought it looked like a duck.

186
00:10:49,620 --> 00:10:51,060
So they called it the Ruptured Duck.

187
00:10:51,920 --> 00:10:54,268
But getting home was only the start.

188
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:56,580
Millions of veterans
needed jobs and homes.

189
00:10:56,880 --> 00:11:00,140
Even clothes were scarce.
That story is next.

190
00:11:00,340 --> 00:11:01,560
Subscribe so you don't miss it.
